<title>NETHERLANDS: Euro cannot go ahead without Germany - Duisenberg.</title>
<headline>Euro cannot go ahead without Germany - Duisenberg.</headline>
<dateline>NOORDWIJK, Netherlands 1997-06-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>Europe's single currency, the euro, cannot go ahead without Germany, Dutch central bank president Wim Duisenberg said on Friday.</p>
<p>Duisenberg takes over on July 1 as head of the European Monetary Institute, the forerunner of the European Central Bank, which is scheduled to run monetary policy under economic and monetary union (EMU) from 1999.</p>
<p>Asked if the euro could be launched without Germany, he told reporters: &quot;I don't think it would make much sense to have a common currency area without Germany and France.&quot;</p>
<p>Duisenberg said that Germany, France, Spain, Portugal and Italy had made great efforts to meet the entry requirements for monetary union.</p>
<p>He was speaking to reporters before addressing a conference on global economic convergence in the Dutch seaside resort of Noordwijk.</p>
